Java Bootcamp — Lab Environment
Everything runs on one shared server in AWS (us-west-2, IP 100.22.136.97). Students work from desktop VS Code on their laptops and connect out to these services — nothing to install server-side, no SSH needed for students.
Shared services

PostgreSQL 17 — 100.22.136.97:5432, database bootcamp. Each user gets their own role + schema (default search_path = their schema); users can't see each other's schemas.
Apache Kafka 4.0 — bootstrap 100.22.136.97:9092. Single broker, shared across the cohort (no per-user topic isolation).
Kubernetes (k3s) — API https://100.22.136.97:6443. Each user gets their own namespace with admin rights in it; Traefik ingress on :80/:443 for routes. Locked to their namespace.
Source/CI: GitHub + GitHub Actions. Container images: GHCR.
What each student gets (one row in the credentials sheet + one kubeconfig file):

DB: jdbc:postgresql://100.22.136.97:5432/bootcamp?currentSchema=studentNN, user studentNN + password
Kafka: 100.22.136.97:9092
K8s: kubeconfigs/studentNN.yaml (kubectl from laptop works out of the box)
Student laptop (Lab 0): desktop VS Code + JDK 21, Maven 3.9.x, Node 22, Git. Point application.properties at the endpoints above. Deploy labs: build image → push to GHCR (or build on the server) → kubectl apply into your namespace.
Your access: full AWS account admin (separate login I'll send) — resize the box, open ports, restart containers, adjust anything. Shell into the box via EC2 Instance Connect from the console.
Notes: it's a baseline you can tweak. Sized for 15 concurrent on one box; DB/Kafka are plaintext on a firewall-restricted network (class IPs only). Per the confirmed spec it's PostgreSQL + GitHub Actions + k3s (not Oracle/Bitbucket/OpenShift).